=== task: mcq medium
title: Why is it stable?
prompt: Why does bubble sort keep equal items in their original order?
* It swaps only when the left item is strictly bigger, so equal items never pass each other :: Right. Two equal neighbours are never swapped.
- It compares every pair of items in the list :: It compares neighbours; that isn't what keeps equal items in order.
- It sorts in place :: Some in-place sorts aren't stable, like selection sort, coming next.
- It always makes n − 1 passes :: The number of passes has nothing to do with it, and the early stop can make fewer.
hint: When would two equal items change places?
hint: Look at the condition for a swap.
right: Items only move by swapping with a neighbour, and equal neighbours are never swapped, so one equal item can never jump over another.
wrong: The swap condition is `>`, not `>=`. Equal items never swap, and since items only move one place at a time, they can't get past each other.

=== task: predict medium
